  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 940 PM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHigh pressure to our east flattens as a trough moves across the\nnorthern high plains and southern Canada. The only real\nsignificant change for us will be flow shifting from\nsouthwesterly to more north through northwesterly. Models\ncontinue to indicate drier air across the area through Thursday\nand with no disturbances, anticipate dry conditions for the\nentire CWA. Warm temperatures will continue as highs remain\nanywhere from 5 to 10 degrees above more seasonal values.\n\nSome deeper mixing will bring some gusty winds to the surface\ntoday and tomorrow while humidities hover around 10 to 15%.\nHowever, the gusty winds will be marginal at best. So despite \nthese values being close to critical fire weather thresholds, \nonly localized critical fire weather conditions are expected \nmainly for the Four Corners region. \n\nAs far as our next moisture push, models continue to highlight\nPWATs reaching upwards of 150% of normal for the Friday\nafternoon onwards. This remains dependent on the evolution and\ntrack of an area of low pressure/trough off the PacNW coast and\nthe resultant moisture advection thanks to a more southerly wind\nflow. If this pans out, we could see some showers and a few\nstorms. Time will tell, however, so keep an eye on the latest \nforecast if you have any big weekend plans.",
